The {{nihongo|'''Shitayanagi Line'''|下柳線|Shitayanagi-sen}} is a [[rapid transit]] line operated by [[Okami Metro Co., Ltd.]] in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]]. Designated as '''Line 9''' of the Okami Metro network, it is identified by the color '''gold''' ({{HTML color|#ffd700}}) and carries the line code '''S'''. The line runs from {{nihongo|Kita|北}} in the north to {{nihongo|Kaido-cho|街道町}} in the southeast, spanning {{cvt|50.5|km|mi|0}} and serving 36 stations. It is one of the longest lines in the Okami Metro system.

The Shitayanagi Line is built to [[narrow gauge]] of {{RailGauge|1067mm|allk=on}} with {{1,500 V DC}} overhead catenary, a legacy of its original survey along a former suburban railway right-of-way (a gauge specification shared within the Okami Metro only with the [[Nanboku Line (Okami)|Nanboku Line]] and [[Tokuyama Line]]). While the Tokuyama Line uses its narrow-gauge alignment for direct through-running with [[Okaiken Railways]], the Shitayanagi Line operates as a self-contained cross-city trunk line.

The line serves as a major cross-city corridor, connecting the northern wards of Kita and Tatara with the central business districts of Chiyoda and Chuo, before continuing southeast through the Minami, Terao, and Kawauchi wards. It offers interchanges with 12 other metro lines, including major transfer hubs at [[Okami Chuo (S)|Okami Chuo]] (with the [[Miyoshi Line]] and [[Chūō Line (Okami)|Chūō Line]]) and Kachidoki (with the [[Imaichi Line]] and [[Tedorigawa Line]]). In a recent modernization effort, the station formerly known as Nishi-Tatara was renamed {{nihongo|Sogo-taiikukan-mae|総合体育館前||"Prefectural Sports Complex"}}, reflecting the development of a major sports facility in the area.

==History==

===Planning and construction===

The Shitayanagi Line was conceived in the late 1960s as part of the third phase of expansion of the Okami Municipal Subway. Following the [[Great Adonian War]], Okami experienced rapid population growth and suburbanization, particularly in the northern and southeastern districts. The existing tram and early subway lines were insufficient to handle the surge in commuter traffic, and the city government identified a pressing need for a new cross-city corridor.

Construction began in 1970. The alignment was originally surveyed along a former suburban railway right-of-way that had been built to {{RailGauge|1067mm|allk=on}} narrow gauge standards during the early 20th century. Although the municipal subway system had by then adopted [[standard gauge]] of {{RailGauge|sg|allk=on}} for its newer lines, the decision was made to retain the narrow gauge for the Shitayanagi Line to reduce construction costs and expedite the opening of the line. This decision would later prove consequential, as it precluded the line from through-service operations with other metro lines.

===Opening and early operation===

The first segment of the Shitayanagi Line opened on March 15, 1974, between Kita and Okami Chuo, covering approximately 20 kilometers and 15 stations. The line was an immediate success, carrying over 300,000 passengers daily within its first year of operation. The opening coincided with a period of rapid economic expansion in Okaiken, and the line proved instrumental in connecting the growing residential districts of northern Okami with the city center.

The second phase of construction extended the line southeast from Okami Chuo through the Tama and Minami wards, reaching Kaido-cho in 1979. This extension brought the total line length to its current {{cvt|50.5|km|mi|0}} and added 21 new stations, including several in the developing Terao and Kawauchi districts.

===Modernization===

In the 2000s, the Shitayanagi Line underwent a program of station modernization, with older stations receiving escalator and elevator installations to improve accessibility. The line was one of the first in the network to achieve full [[barrier-free]] compliance under the "One Route" initiative.

Following the privatization of the [[Okami Municipal Transportation Bureau]] in 2018, the line received new 6000 series rolling stock manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]], replacing the aging 2000 series trains that had been in service since the line's opening. The 2000 series fleet, which had been refurbished in 2018, is being progressively phased out and is expected to be fully retired by 2027.

In 2023, the station formerly known as Nishi-Tatara was renamed {{nihongo|Sogo-taiikukan-mae|総合体育館前}}, following the completion of the Okami Prefectural Sports Complex in the adjacent Nishi ward. The renaming was part of a broader effort to align station names with prominent local landmarks.

