==Rolling stock==

The Shitayanagi Line operates '''Type C''' rolling stock, consisting of 6-car trains approximately {{cvt|120|m|ft|0}} in length with a car width of {{cvt|2.8|m|ft|0}}. The line's narrow gauge alignment requires specialized rolling stock that cannot be interchanged with standard-gauge lines.

;Current fleet
* {{nihongo|'''6000 series'''|6000系}} — Introduced in 2020, manufactured by [[Okami Heavy Industries]]. These trains form the current backbone of the Shitayanagi Line fleet, featuring modern LED lighting, accessibility improvements, and energy-efficient propulsion systems. The 6000 series was designed specifically for narrow-gauge operation and is shared with the [[Tedorigawa Line]] and [[Tozai Line (Okami)|Tozai Line]].

;Legacy fleet
* {{nihongo|'''2000 series'''|2000系}} — Introduced in 1981, originally built for the Shitayanagi and Tedorigawa Lines. The fleet was refurbished in 2018 to extend its service life, but is now being progressively phased out in favor of the 6000 series. As of 2026, only a handful of 2000 series sets remain in revenue service, and full retirement is expected by 2027.

==Through-service==

Unlike most other Okami Metro lines, the Shitayanagi Line does not operate through-service with any other line or railway operator. Because the Shitayanagi Line is built to {{RailGauge|1067mm|allk=on}} narrow gauge rather than the {{RailGauge|sg|allk=on}} standard gauge used by Okami's private railways and the Tozai and Tedorigawa lines, through-running with the standard-gauge private network is physically impossible without gauge-changing equipment. Consequently, the Shitayanagi Line operates as a self-contained line, with all services terminating at either Kita or Kaido-cho.

